## APPLY STRICT ANALYTICAL BOUNDARIES

ISZE billing measures ISZE-to-distributor purchasing.

Distributor-to-dealer sales measure a different sales stage.

Dealer-to-end-customer sales measure another stage.

Never combine them as though they are interchangeable.

Missing sales-to-dealer information means UNKNOWN, not zero.

A missing dealer purchase in an incomplete extract is not proof that the dealer buys from competitors.

Warranty records failures, claims and settlements. It is not reliable evidence of scheduled maintenance, filter replacement, brake servicing, PMS capture or dealer servicing failure.

UIO and vehicle parc describe exposure and potential-demand context. They are not realised sales.

Modelled expected service demand is not measured market demand.

A modelled shortfall is not automatically lost sales.

Billing has no inherent VIN, chassis or vehicle-model attribution. Attribute sales to a specific model or tonnage band only through validated exclusive applicability.

Pool multi-band and multi-family parts rather than allocating them by fleet share.

Keep recorded exclusive-applicability purchasing, modelled expected demand and market-level purchasing separate.

Do not sum repeated market actuals across model rows or divide them by one band’s expected demand to claim band-level service coverage.

Use one reporting clock and period at a time. Never add calendar-year and financial-year totals.

Preserve the distinction between GP, GPS and PMS. They are not interchangeable concepts.

Use the approved source definitions and document any unresolved terminology.


## REVIEW COMMERCIAL CAUSALITY

A sales decline is a signal, not a diagnosis.

A competitor price disadvantage is evidence of positioning, not proof that a price cut will recover sales.

A lower Genuine total does not necessarily mean that the combined Genuine/BVP business is deteriorating.

A larger vehicle parc does not automatically mean that purchases should increase proportionally.

Require alternative explanations to be considered, including:

Supply constraints.
Backorders.
Distributor stock timing.
Supersession.
Product mix.
BVP migration.
Large one-off orders.
SPA expiry.
Tender effects.
Seasonality.
Changes in applicable vehicles.
Incomplete source coverage.
Changes in recorded account populations.

The paper should explain how Universe helps narrow these explanations rather than pretending to know the cause automatically.

Where several independent signals support an interpretation, describe the resulting confidence in plain language.

Do not convert a judgment into a precise numerical confidence score without a defensible calibration method.


