## DEALER PARTICIPATION: DESIGN THE VALUE EXCHANGE EXPLICITLY

Dealer participation deserves particular attention.

Do not assume that an engaged distributor automatically creates engaged dealers.

Also do not assume that every dealer must upload data directly.

Some dealer-level analysis may already be possible through distributor-to-dealer data. Dealer participation might consist of receiving a useful review, validating local context, agreeing an action and reporting an outcome.

Avoid duplicating data collection where the distributor already provides the relevant information.

For the dealer, explain:

What they receive.
How frequently they receive it.
How much work participation requires.
Which information they must provide, if any.
Which information remains optional.
Who sees their information.
What practical decision becomes easier.
Why they would continue after a pilot or incentive ends.

The first useful output should not require months of participation or a perfect dataset.

The value proposition should not be merely:

“You help ISZE understand the market.”

It must include a credible benefit for the dealer itself, delivered through an appropriate relationship with the distributor.


